# Findings gitleaks reports that are not secrets. Every one was read before it was listed here.
#
# All of them are FIXTURES: values that exist so a test can prove `redact_secrets` removes them. Listing
# them is not silencing the scanner. It is the only way `scripts/gates.sh security` can ever be
# green, and a gate nobody can make green is a gate nobody runs -- which is how the six sat unread
# from 2026-08-08 until somebody finally ran the leg.
#
# A fingerprint is `commit:file:rule:line`, pinned to the commit that INTRODUCED the line. That is
# the safety property and not an accident: edit one of these fixtures in a future commit and
# gitleaks reports a NEW fingerprint and goes red again, wanting a fresh pair of eyes. This file
# forgives the past. It cannot forgive the future.
#
# To re-read them:
#   gitleaks detect --redact --no-banner --report-format json --report-path leaks.json

# The literal `Sec-WebSocket-Key` from RFC 6455, section 1.3 -- base64 of the words "the sample
# nonce". It is printed in the specification and in every handshake example ever written, and it
# authenticates nothing: the header is a framing check against caching proxies, not a credential.
d235a7b427a48c5dccb25978d97f7deb2c0719e1:sidecars/browser/fence/proxy_test.go:generic-api-key:120

# Three PEM blocks whose body is one letter repeated -- `MIIEowIBAAKCAQEAaaaaaaaa...`. No modulus,
# no key material, nothing that decrypts anything. Each is the INPUT to a test asserting the block
# comes back redacted, and the last two exist because of bugs found in the real redactor: an
# encrypted key's RFC 1421 headers used to end the body early, leaving the whole key in the text
# under a marker saying it had been removed; and a footerless key used to borrow the footer of an
# unrelated block further down, swallowing a message between them.
